      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "1. Each digit can be represented by 4, 9, or 2.\n\n\\(\\begin{aligned} f_{\\text{total}}(4, 9, 2) &= 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\ &= 9 \\end{aligned}\\)\n\n2. The code to compute the 4-digit numbers is as follows:\n\n\\(\\begin{aligned} text{Function: func}\\\\ var_declaration: int[] \\\\ foreach: int value in 4 \\\\ end foreach\\\\ foreach: int value in 9 \\\\ end foreach\\\\ foreach: int value in 2 \\\\ end foreach\\\\ return 0 \\\\ end function \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\)\n\nRationale:\nTo solve this problem, we must consider the various permutations of digits (2, 2, 9, and 9) to form four-digit integers. Each unique digit within the set 2, 2, 9, and 9 can contribute to a total of 4, 9, and 2 unique values, respectively. Therefore, using the combinatorial formula \\(n_k\\) (permutations), we obtain \\(n^k\\) ways in which we can pick \\(k\\) unique numbers from a set size \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{\\text{total}}(4, 9, 2) &= 3 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nHence, there are \\boxed{9} different positive integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9.",
