      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "We understand that each of the numbers 2, 2, 9 and 9 has two identical digits. Therefore, consider the following possibilities:\n- Choosing one 2 and one 9 may yield sets `(2, 9, 2, 9)` or `(9, 2, 2, 9)` or one 2 and one 2 and two 9s may produce `2 (2 9s, 9 2s, 2 9s, 9 2s)`, giving us `C(2, 1) * C(2, 1, 1) * C(2, 2) = 3`.\n- Letting the 9s be chosen together with the 2s, then choosing 1 each to form `(9, 2, 9, 2) or (2, 9, 9, 2)` will give us `C(2, 1, 1, 2) = 2`.\n\nHence, combining all scenarios, the total number of ways to form the required four-digit integer is `3 + 2 = 5`. Therefore, the total number of different positive, four-digit integers that can be formed from the digits 2, 2, 9 and 9 is $\\boxed{5}$.",
